/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (The SQL Injection exercises demonstrated how improperly handled user input can alter database queries and result in unauthorized authentication or access to data. PortSwigger notes that SQL Injection can allow attackers to interfere with application database queries and potentially retrieve or modify data.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (The XSS exercise demonstrated how user-controlled input can be reflected into an HTML response and interpreted as executable browser content.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (The unprotected administrative functionality exercise demonstrated the security risk of exposing privileged functionality without appropriate access controls.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 98 0 R
/E (The File Path Traversal exercise demonstrated how manipulating a file-related parameter can allow unauthorized access to files on the server. PortSwigger describes path traversal as a vulnerability that can enable attackers to read arbitrary files from the application server.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
