171 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (The PortSwigger Web Security Academy project provided practical experience in identifying, exploiting, and understanding common web application vulnerabilities. Five Apprentice-level labs were successfully completed, covering SQL Injection, Reflected XSS, Unprotected Administrative Functionality, and File Path Traversal.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
172 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (The exercises demonstrated that improper input handling, insufficient access controls, and insecure interaction with databases or filesystem resources can expose web applications to significant security risks.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
173 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (The project also provided hands-on experience with Burp Suite for analyzing and modifying HTTP requests. The successful completion of the labs helped strengthen practical understanding of web application security testing and the importance of implementing appropriate defensive controls.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 100 0 R
/E (Overall, the project fulfilled its objective of providing practical exposure to web vulnerability assessment in a controlled and authorized environment. Screenshots and lab completion evidence have been retained as supporting proof for the technical report.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
